            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house is pretty good for air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed. 

The TV cable coming through the wall. I recommend to outfit electrical outlets/switches/cables located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend adding weather stripping to your front exterior door. The front door appears to make poor contact with the framing (I assume due to the shifting). Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, otherwise this door/frame may need to be adjusted/replaced. Ensure the weather stripping makes good contact when the door is closed, an adjustable strike plate may help to ensure a tight closed position. 

As you mentioned there is some roofing work coming up in the near future, I recommend to investigate those two areas we found along the support beam above the living/dining room. 

There was a significant amount of air leakage observed along the baseboards in both lower bedrooms. Ideally, this would be sealed up from the crawlspace, however the master bedroom could be done by removing the baseboard trim and sealing from the inside (as space is limited in the crawlspace area underneath). 

Although minimal, air leakage was also observed through the two bathroom exhaust fans located on the main and upper floors. When you decide to replace these, I recommend you replace them with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 140 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.5 GJ, and qualify for up to$ 500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within three months of the second energy evaluation. For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $ 1,100 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25.8 GJ, and qualify for up to$ 1,875 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The cathedral ceiling of your house was assumed to have 9 inches (R32) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R24) rigid foam, polyisocyanurate insulation.



Savings:

By adding 4 inches (R24) of rigid foam, polyisocyanurate insulation to the cathedral ceiling, you could potentially save $ 220 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.2 GJ. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilingsCathedral>
                <HotWaterSystem>

I have modelled the potential savings associated with the Toyotomi OM-122DW, as you have this model on order to be installed to replace your current hot water heater, 



Savings:

By replacing your current hot water heater with the Toyotomi OM-122DW, you could potentially save $ 70 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.8 GJ. 



Please note, the Toyotomi OM-122DW is currently under going tests by NRCan under the latest standards of testing for efficiency. I will update your report to reflect these results once testing has concluded/released. This testing will also determine if this product is eligible for a rebate through the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 80% AFUE. I have modeled an upgrade to the Conforto KHM-100, as you currently have this on order to replace your exisiting furnace. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with the Conforto KHM-100, with an efficiency of 86.8% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 430 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.0 GJ. An oil fired furnace must have a rated AFUE of 92% or higher to qualify for a rebate. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

COMBUSTION AIR

During the assesment, there was a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the furnace and hot water tank, however the dryer vent was installed through this pipe blocking combustion air from entering the room freely, 

I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your furnace and hot water tank are satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the furnace or hot water tank is operating.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



WOODSTOVE

Savings (with current oil fired furnace):

By using your current wood stove for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 1,600 per year in energy savings. This assumes a cost of $ 450 per cord of wood. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh, $0.72 per kWh after subsity limit

Oil  - $1.63 per litre 

Propane - $1.52 per litre

Nat Gas - $37.21 per GJ

Wood - $551 per cord 

Pellets - $8.39 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
